[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Access port VLAN membership on switch1 (Campus 1 access switch), specifically interface eth2 (PC5’s access port).
- Mechanism: PC5’s frames are being placed in VLAN 1 instead of VLAN 10, so ARP requests for its default gateway 172.16.10.254 (which lives in VLAN 10) never reach the gateway. Other hosts (PC6/PC7/PC8) are correctly in VLAN 10, so their traffic traverses the trunk to mswitch0 and reaches the gateway normally.
- Localization: switch1.eth2 is enslaved to br_vlan1, while switch1.eth3-eth5 are enslaved to br_vlan10. Evidence: on switch1 “ip addr” shows eth2 master br_vlan1, and eth3/4/5 master br_vlan10. From PC6, ping to PC5 yields “Destination Host Unreachable” with ARP “incomplete” for 172.16.10.5, while ping to 172.16.10.254 succeeds. On mswitch0, vlan10 is up with IP 172.16.10.254 and serving other hosts, confirming the trunk path for VLAN 10 works.
- Root cause: Misconfiguration of switch1 port eth2 to VLAN 1 instead of VLAN 10 for PC5’s access port.
- Corrective fix: Move switch1.eth2 from br_vlan1 to br_vlan10 (i.e., assign PC5’s access port to VLAN 10). No fault found on switch4; its trunk is unrelated to Campus 1 VLAN 10. After reassigning eth2 to VLAN 10, PC5 should ARP its gateway and regain connectivity.
=== END ===